<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catilinarian conspiracy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">63–62 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Second Catilinarian conspiracy, Conspiracy of Catiline</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Catilinarian conspiracy was a plot by the Roman aristocrat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catiline">Lucius Sergius Catilina</a> (Catiline) and his associates to seize power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 63 BCE, after he had again failed to win election as consul. Exposed by the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, whose four speeches against Catiline became classics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> oratory, the conspiracy collapsed: Catiline left the city to join a rebel army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, and five conspirators arrested in Rome were executed without trial after a debate in the Senate. Catiline was killed when his army was destroyed near Pistoria early in 62 BCE.</p></div>
